{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"**Generally, APs aren’t paid by the ETF sponsor simply for being APs.** They typically earn trading spreads, arbitrage profits, or fees from clients whose creations/redemptions they facilitate. APs often **pay** creation/redemption transaction fees. Specific commercial arrangements can differ.\n\n**No, APs generally aren’t required to keep creating or redeeming.** Their agreements authorize those transactions; they usually don’t impose a continuous obligation to transact or support the ETF’s price. An AP can step back if trading becomes unattractive or risky, although accepted orders carry contractual settlement obligations.\n\n**Worth knowing:** An AP and a market maker are different roles, even when one firm performs both. Any exchange-imposed quoting obligations attach to the market-making role—not automatically to AP status."}}
